refactor(backup): take the backup as a network client, not through the runtime (#237)

The backup reached Postgres and the object store through the container runtime:
ten `docker exec`, two `docker cp` and a `docker run`. A process holding that
socket is root on the machine, and the backup is about to become a service that
is up as long as the stack is. None of those calls survive.

What replaces them is smaller, not larger. `pg_dump -h` writes the dump
straight to disk, so both `docker cp` calls simply disappear; createdb,
pg_restore, psql and dropdb take `-h`; and the segment fetch is one `aws s3 cp`
from an image that is already on the object network instead of a container
launched to be on it.

Two details are measured rather than assumed, and both are written down where
they bite:

  --no-owner --no-acl on the restore. The dump's objects belong to the schema
  owner and this role is not a superuser, so it cannot become them; without the
  flags every ALTER ... OWNER TO in the dump fails and --exit-on-error stops.

  PGOPTIONS default_transaction_read_only=off for the restore half only. The
  role carries the read-only default and CREATE DATABASE is refused 25006
  without this — which says nothing about the privilege and reads exactly like
  a missing GRANT.

The image is a new Dockerfile target rather than the runtime one: the backup is
the only row that wants a Postgres client and an S3 client, and both now come
from one pinned base so there is a single digest to audit.

The selftest runs the script IN that image, sharing the ledger container's
network namespace — so it creates no network, keeps the ~29-network ceiling
intact, and tests the command the service will actually run rather than
whatever client happens to be installed on the machine. That change was forced
by measurement: the first run of the rewritten selftest failed nine cases on a
machine with no psql, which from the outside looks identical to an unreachable
ledger.

Measured end to end against the deployment: 877KB dump, restore held, 2137
events read back, 34 sealed segments all agreeing. 12/12 in the selftest.

Driven by OPS-045, with BAK-001..BAK-004 rewired to the network interface.

